Chapter: ParticlesKernel
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceEffect

 A GPU compute particle system as a [Device] kernel: 20000 particles, one per
 thread, each pulled by two gravity wells that orbit the centre. Each thread
 reads its particle's state (px, py, vx, vy) from an input buffer, accumulates
 a softened inverse-square attraction toward both wells, integrates with light
 damping, wraps toroidally, and writes the new state to an output buffer. The
 host ping-pongs the two buffers and draws speed-coloured additive points. This
 is the reference site's "compute particles" technique, from Codex [Device] AST.

 All fixed-point integer arithmetic (positions in pixels, velocity in 1/16
 pixels) so the buffer stays i32 and matches the host exactly. No neighbour
 reads -- each particle sees only its own state and the two analytic wells.

 We say:

Section: Screen

  pa-width : Integer = 1024
  pa-height : Integer = 768

Section: Fixed-Point Trig

  pa-wrap : Integer -> Integer
  pa-wrap (a) = let m = a - (a / 6283) * 6283 in if m < 0 then m + 6283 else m

  pa-sin-core : Integer -> Integer
  pa-sin-core (x) =
    let x2 = x * x / 1000
    in let x3 = x2 * x / 1000
    in let x5 = x3 * x2 / 1000
    in x - x3 / 6 + x5 / 120

  pa-sin : Integer -> Integer
  pa-sin (raw) =
    let a = pa-wrap raw
    in if a <= 1570 then pa-sin-core a
    else if a <= 3141 then pa-sin-core (3141 - a)
    else if a <= 4712 then 0 - pa-sin-core (a - 3141)
    else 0 - pa-sin-core (6283 - a)

  pa-cos : Integer -> Integer
  pa-cos (raw) = pa-sin (raw + 1570)

Section: Gravity Wells

 Two wells orbit the centre in antiphase. Angle advances with the frame.

  pa-ax0 : Integer -> Integer
  pa-ax0 (frame) = 512 + pa-cos (frame * 34) * 270 / 1000

  pa-ay0 : Integer -> Integer
  pa-ay0 (frame) = 384 + pa-sin (frame * 34) * 180 / 1000

  pa-ax1 : Integer -> Integer
  pa-ax1 (frame) = 512 - pa-cos (frame * 34) * 270 / 1000

  pa-ay1 : Integer -> Integer
  pa-ay1 (frame) = 384 - pa-sin (frame * 34) * 180 / 1000

 Softened attraction toward a well: acceleration component along one axis.
 inv falls off with distance-squared; the divide keeps it bounded near a well.

  pa-acc : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pa-acc (px) (py) (ax) (ay) =
    let dx = ax - px
    in let dy = ay - py
    in let d2 = dx * dx + dy * dy
    in let inv = 260000 / (d2 / 256 + 500)
    in dx * inv / 11000

  pa-acc-y : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pa-acc-y (px) (py) (ax) (ay) =
    let dx = ax - px
    in let dy = ay - py
    in let d2 = dx * dx + dy * dy
    in let inv = 260000 / (d2 / 256 + 500)
    in dy * inv / 11000

Section: Wrap

  pa-wrap-pos : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pa-wrap-pos (v) (m) = let r = v - (v / m) * m in if r < 0 then r + m else r

Section: Particle Kernel

 One thread per particle. Read state from inb, integrate under both wells,
 write advanced state to outb.

  particles-step : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device] Integer
  particles-step (inb) (outb) (frame) (gid) = act
    px <- device-load inb (gid * 4)
    py <- device-load inb (gid * 4 + 1)
    vx <- device-load inb (gid * 4 + 2)
    vy <- device-load inb (gid * 4 + 3)
    let ax0 = pa-ax0 frame
    in let ay0 = pa-ay0 frame
    in let ax1 = pa-ax1 frame
    in let ay1 = pa-ay1 frame
    in let accx = pa-acc px py ax0 ay0 + pa-acc px py ax1 ay1
    in let accy = pa-acc-y px py ax0 ay0 + pa-acc-y px py ax1 ay1
    in let nvx = vx - vx / 48 + accx
    in let nvy = vy - vy / 48 + accy
    in let npx = pa-wrap-pos (px + nvx / 16) pa-width
    in let npy = pa-wrap-pos (py + nvy / 16) pa-height
    in act
    s0 <- device-store outb (gid * 4) npx
    s1 <- device-store outb (gid * 4 + 1) npy
    s2 <- device-store outb (gid * 4 + 2) nvx
    device-store outb (gid * 4 + 3) nvy
    end
  end
